      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127777,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 302,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T11:59:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127778,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 302,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Papillary haemangioma is characterized by dermal, widely dilated, thin-walled vascular channels with numerous luminal papillary projections containing capillaries lined by plump endothelial cells, surrounded by pericytes, and displaying frequent intracytoplasmic eosinophilic globules. These globules, known as thanatosomes, represent dilated lysosomes containing debris and fat vacuoles. Endothelial cells are positive for CD31, CD34, and ERG and are negative for lymphatic markers.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>The lesions are histologically similar to glomeruloid haemangiomas, which differ in their typical multifocally, involvement of the trunk and extremities, and association with POEMS syndrome.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T11:59:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
